WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
Question 0030: the spider and the fly,
a junior-level question.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:10.100
Two parts: the shortest way over the box,
then the idea of code that checks every net.

3
00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:14.800
A room shaped like a box: 30 long,
12 wide and 12 high.

4
00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:19.333
The spider S is on an end wall, mid-width,
one unit below the ceiling.

5
00:00:19.333 --> 00:00:23.933
The fly F is on the opposite wall, mid-width,
one unit above the floor.

6
00:00:23.933 --> 00:00:29.933
The spider walks only on the surfaces, not through the air.
What is the shortest way to the fly?

7
00:00:30.733 --> 00:00:35.467
Part A: what is the shortest distance,
and where does the spider pass from face to face?

8
00:00:36.967 --> 00:00:42.200
The route that comes to mind first: up to the ceiling,
along the whole ceiling, and down to the fly.

9
00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:47.433
Its length: 1 + 30 + 11 = 42.
The first answer that comes to mind, and it is wrong.

10
00:00:47.833 --> 00:00:53.433
Unfold the three faces into a plane, like cardboard:
the route becomes a straight line of length 42.

11
00:00:53.433 --> 00:00:57.433
Exactly the same length over the floor: 11 + 30 + 1.
But maybe other faces give a shorter line?

12
00:01:00.433 --> 00:01:06.833
Over one of the long walls: unfold
both end walls onto the far wall.

13
00:01:06.833 --> 00:01:11.633
Both are 6 from the fold, at heights 11 and 1:
the line is longer, 43.17.

14
00:01:14.633 --> 00:01:21.233
Now five faces, as stairs: the spider's wall,
the ceiling, the far wall, the floor and the fly's wall.

15
00:01:21.233 --> 00:01:25.333
In this net the straight line from S to F
crosses all five faces.

16
00:01:25.333 --> 00:01:30.567
S and F are corners of a right triangle.
Across: 1 + 30 + 1 = 32.

17
00:01:30.567 --> 00:01:35.300
And up: half the ceiling, the far wall, half the floor,
6 + 12 + 6 = 24.

18
00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:40.333
Pythagoras: the root of 1,024 + 576,
that is, the root of 1,600: exactly 40.

19
00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:45.833
The line is valid: it crosses every fold inside the edge,
at x = 7 and x = 23, through the edges.

20
00:01:46.433 --> 00:01:51.833
Fold it back, and the straight line
becomes a route over the box.

21
00:01:51.833 --> 00:01:55.833
It climbs its wall to the ceiling, crosses it
diagonally to the far wall, and goes down to the floor.

22
00:01:55.833 --> 00:02:01.233
From there diagonally over the floor to the fly's wall,
and up to the fly. Five faces, not three.

23
00:02:01.233 --> 00:02:06.133
The lengths on the faces: 1.25, 8.75, 20,
8.75 and 1.25. Together 40.

24
00:02:06.133 --> 00:02:12.133
And a mirror image: the same route over the near wall,
also exactly 40.

25
00:02:12.933 --> 00:02:18.467
Every unfolding on the page: over three faces,
four or five, each with its length.

26
00:02:18.467 --> 00:02:23.933
The shortest is 40, twice: our route
and its mirror image, over the near wall.

27
00:02:23.933 --> 00:02:27.533
And 42, the route that comes first,
is only in seventh place.

28
00:02:27.533 --> 00:02:32.567
And a lower bound: through the air, 31.6.
No route over the faces is shorter.

29
00:02:33.367 --> 00:02:38.567
Part B: a function for any box and any two points,
without handling each net on its own.

30
00:02:39.267 --> 00:02:44.667
Every order of faces, from wall to wall, is one unfolding.
Here are all 16 unfoldings on the page.

31
00:02:44.667 --> 00:02:49.867
Each with its straight line and its length,
from the shortest to the longest.

32
00:02:49.867 --> 00:02:54.467
The shortest, 40, are marked: our route
and its mirror image.

33
00:02:54.467 --> 00:02:58.933
The longest, 57.27, goes over the floor,
a side wall and the ceiling.

34
00:02:58.933 --> 00:03:04.433
The idea in three steps. Step 1: go
over every order of faces and unfold each into the plane.

35
00:03:04.433 --> 00:03:09.933
Step 2: in each, draw a straight line from S to F,
and check that it stays inside the faces.

36
00:03:09.933 --> 00:03:14.167
Step 3: take the shortest
of the valid lines. Here, 40.

37
00:03:14.167 --> 00:03:18.167
But 16 different unfoldings are 16 cases in code.
The trick: they are all lines in one strip.

38
00:03:19.767 --> 00:03:25.567
The four side faces wrap the box
like a hoop. Unroll them into one strip.

39
00:03:25.567 --> 00:03:31.067
A coordinate p runs around the perimeter: floor,
far wall, ceiling and near wall. The period is 48.

40
00:03:31.067 --> 00:03:36.867
The spider's wall folds onto the strip around
one of its four edges: four images of S.

41
00:03:36.867 --> 00:03:42.367
Each image is S's distance from its edge, left of x = 0.
Over the ceiling, for example: x = −1 and p = 30.

42
00:03:42.367 --> 00:03:46.167
In the same way, four images of F,
right of x = 30.

43
00:03:46.167 --> 00:03:51.367
Each image of F also one period up or down:
4 · 4 · 3 = 48 lines in all.

44
00:03:51.867 --> 00:03:57.333
Each line has two checks: it crosses x = 0
inside the edge the spider folded around,

45
00:03:57.333 --> 00:04:02.833
and x = 30 inside the fly's edge.
The red line leaves at p = 33.57, outside: rejected.

46
00:04:03.333 --> 00:04:08.067
Of the 48 lines, 22 are valid.
These are the ones inside the strip, with no shift.

47
00:04:08.067 --> 00:04:13.667
The shortest: from S over the ceiling to F over the floor.
Δx = 32, Δp = −24, length 40.

48
00:04:13.667 --> 00:04:19.167
The exit point: p = 29.25, inside the ceiling's edge,
that is, the point (0, 6.75, 12), as in part A.

49
00:04:19.167 --> 00:04:24.667
Every net is a line in one strip: 48 lines,
one root and two comparisons each, no special cases.

50
00:04:25.467 --> 00:04:30.633
This is the box on the page: the strip,
the eight images and the valid lines.

51
00:04:30.633 --> 00:04:35.100
With "all 48" you also see the lines
rejected by the two checks.

52
00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:39.600
With "shortest only", the route of 40 remains.
You can drag S and F and change the sizes.

53
00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:44.100
When the spider moves half way down, z = 6,
the straight route over the floor wins: 6 + 30 + 1 = 37.

54
00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:48.600
And at the point (0, 2, 11), near the near wall,
the shortest goes over four faces: 37.12.

55
00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:55.000
To sum up: unfold the faces, and the shortest route
is a straight line over five faces, of length 40.

56
00:04:55.000 --> 00:05:00.900
Drag the spider and the fly in the box
on the page of question 0030 on the site.
